# Build artifacts - everything cargo generates when compiling
# (target/debug, target/release, .fingerprint caches, incremental
# compilation data, etc.). This one line covers all of it, since
# everything cargo produces lives under this single top-level folder.
/target

# NOTE: Cargo.lock is intentionally NOT ignored. For a binary (an
# application, like img2ico) - as opposed to a library - committing
# Cargo.lock is the recommended practice: it pins the exact dependency
# versions this project was built and tested against, so anyone who
# clones the repo gets a reproducible build instead of potentially newer
# (and untested) dependency versions.

# Editor/IDE-specific folders. These hold personal editor settings, not
# project files - everyone who clones this repo may use a different
# editor, and their local settings shouldn't end up in version control.
.vscode/
.idea/
*.iml
.vs/

# OS-generated clutter files - metadata Windows/macOS create automatically
# in every folder, never something a repository should track.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# Rustfmt occasionally leaves a .rs.bk backup file behind when reformatting
# fails partway through.
*.rs.bk

# Windows debug symbol files, generated alongside a build - large and
# regenerable, not something to commit.
*.pdb